<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e6f2f8;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; padding-bottom: 5px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # My Family – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin: 0;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> Pre-K (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> My Family</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> My Family</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From _______ to _______</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Recognize that people are speaking in English. Interactive: Respond to cues in simple rhymes and songs.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can identify family-related words like "mom," "dad," or "me" when spoken (e.g., listening to and repeating family names, along with body movements while singing). Can listen to and join in rhymes and songs about family (e.g., repeating and acting out words related to family members, such as "mother" and "father," while singing the rhymes). Can identify names of family members when spoken (e.g., pointing to the father's picture when they hear the word "father").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling, and Clarification to activate prior knowledge and introduce the theme "My Family" through multi-sensory experiences.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a simple "Hello Song" with gestures (e.g., "Hello, hello, how are you?"). The teacher then shows large, colorful flashcards of "mom," "dad," and "baby." The teacher points to a flashcard and says the word clearly. Students are encouraged to look and listen. (Vocabulary: mom, dad, baby).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up the "mom" flashcard, points to it, and says, "Mom." The teacher then points to herself and says, "I." The teacher combines these, saying, "This is my mom," while pointing to the flashcard, demonstrating clear pronunciation for /m/ in "mom." The teacher models gestures for "happy" and "love" as well (e.g., big smile for happy, hugging arms for love). This is repeated for "dad" and "baby," using TPR.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher uses simple questions and gestures.
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Holding up "mom" card) "Is this mom or dad?" (Teacher pauses, encourages pointing).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to the "baby" card) "Where is baby?" (Teacher looks expectantly for students to point).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content related to the scenario "My Family" through an engaging audio-visual resource tailored for Pre-K.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario "My Family":</b> The teacher presents "The Finger Family Song" (e.g., using an animated video or finger puppets). The song introduces "Daddy finger, daddy finger, where are you?" "Here I am, here I am, how do you do?" for each family member. It provides auditory input for family names and the question "Who is it?" implicitly. The teacher will act out the song using their fingers, associating each finger with a family member.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Animated video of "The Finger Family Song" on a screen, or teacher-made finger puppets for mom, dad, brother, sister, baby. Large picture cards of a diverse "family" (mom, dad, brother, sister, baby, dog, cat). (Grammar: WH-questions "Who is it?"; Vocabulary: mom, dad, brother, sister, baby, family, dog, cat; verbs: live, love, play; adjectives: happy; Possessive adjectives: my).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Recognizing basic family members by name when spoken and responding to simple cues in songs.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Listening:</b> The teacher plays the song once. "Listen to the song! What do you hear? Family! Yes!" (Teacher points to family flashcard).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Target Word Listening:</b> The teacher plays the song again, pausing after each family member is named. "Listen for 'mom'! Where is mom?" (Teacher points to the mom flashcard; students point to the screen or their finger). This is repeated for 'dad,' 'brother' (pronunciation of /b/), 'sister' (phonemic awareness /s/), 'baby'.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher will also model identifying initial sounds, e.g., "Mmm-mom!" and "Sss-sister!" with exaggerated mouth movements.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening. Students practice identifying and associating target vocabulary with visuals and sounds.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Listening for specific family vocabulary (`mom, dad, brother, sister, baby, dog, cat`) and simple phrases (`This is my...`, `Who is it?`). Developing phonemic awareness for initial sounds /m/ and /s/, and pronunciation of /p/ and /b/ in context. (Grammar: Pronouns "I, you"; Possessive adjectives "my"; Vocabulary: mom, dad, brother, sister, baby, dog, cat; verbs: play; adjectives: happy).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: "Listen and Point"</b>
                            <ul style="list-style-type: circle; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher lays out flashcards of mom, dad, brother, sister, baby, dog, cat on the floor or whiteboard.</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher says a word clearly, e.g., "Mom." Students listen and point to the correct flashcard.</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher repeats with other words: "Dad," "Baby," "Brother" (emphasizing /b/), "Sister" (emphasizing /s/), "Dog," "Cat." The teacher also introduces "play" (emphasizing /p/) by miming playing with a toy and saying "I like to play with my dog!" Students listen and point to the dog.</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice: "Listen and Repeat (Choral)"</b>
                            <ul style="list-style-type: circle; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher holds up a flashcard, says the word, and students repeat chorally. E.g., Teacher: "Mom!" Students: "Mom!"</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher then models simple sentences: Teacher: "This is my mom!" Students: "This is my mom!" (while pointing to the picture). This is repeated for other family members.</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher focuses on exaggerating initial sounds for phonemic awareness: "Mmm-mom," "Sss-sister."</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction: "Listen and Find"</b>
                            <ul style="list-style-type: circle; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are paired up with a small set of family flashcards (mom, dad, baby).</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A says a family word (e.g., "Dad"), and Student B listens and points to the correct card. They switch roles. (Teacher circulates, listens for pronunciation, and provides support, e.g., "Good listening!").</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher might interject with "Who is it?" and prompt students to point to the correct card and say the word if they can, or just point.</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students use listening skills in a realistic scenario related to "My Family." Focus on fluent receptive identification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task: "My Family Photo Gallery" (Guided Listening Response)</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Whole-class guided interaction using individual student family drawings or provided simple pictures.
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Before class, students are asked to draw a picture of their family or are provided with simple cut-out pictures of family members.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher collects the drawings/pictures and holds them up one by one.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher asks the class, "Who is this?" (pointing to a figure in the picture, e.g., the mom).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students listen and respond by pointing to the "mom" in the picture, or by saying "Mom" if they are able. The teacher guides them, "Yes! That's mom!"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher continues for different family members (dad, brother, sister, baby, dog, cat). "Who is in your family?" (Pragmatic: Naming family members).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher encourages using respectful terms when referring to family members (Sociolinguistic: "Mom and Dad").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">If a student shows their own drawing, the teacher can ask "Who is in *your* family?" and encourage the student to point to and name (or just point to) their mom, dad, etc.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> This stage emphasizes listening for identification and responsive interaction within the "My Family" theme. It allows for basic receptive and limited productive use of target vocabulary. (Vocabulary: mom, dad, brother, sister, baby, family, dog, cat; Grammar: "Who is it?", "my").</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment to gauge students' listening comprehension of family vocabulary and self-reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task: "Listen and Point/Circle"</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students receive a simple worksheet with three large pictures: a mom, a dad, and a baby.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher gives instructions: "Listen and point to the mom." Then, "Listen and circle the dad." Finally, "Listen and put an 'X' on the baby."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Vocabulary: mom, dad, baby).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can identify (point to/circle) family members (mom, dad, baby) when spoken.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can respond appropriately to "Who is it?" with a point or simple word.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Thumbs up if you know 'mom'!" (Students show thumbs up/down).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Thumbs up if you know 'dad'!" (Students show thumbs up/down).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student and teacher reflection on the lesson, identifying areas of success and opportunities for improvement, and connecting to the next lesson.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection Prompt:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher asks while showing flashcards) "What new family word did you hear today?" (Students can point to a card or say the word if they remember).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was your favorite family song or picture?" (Students can hum part of the song or point to a picture).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in engaging students and developing their listening comprehension for family vocabulary? (e.g., TPR, songs, flashcards).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What specific sounds or words were challenging for students to distinguish or repeat? (e.g., /b/ in "brother" vs. /p/ in "play").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How can I adjust activities or provide additional scaffolds to reinforce listening for specific words and simple phrases in the next lesson? (e.g., more visual cues, slower pronunciation, more repetition).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Next Lesson:</b> In Lesson 2, students will further develop their receptive skills by engaging with simple visual texts (picture stories or word cards) about "My Family," reinforcing the same vocabulary introduced in this listening lesson to build early reading comprehension.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">2-3 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>